.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt LSEEK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lseek ,
.Nm llseek
.Nd change an open file's offset
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft off_t
.Fn lseek "int fd" "off_t offset" "int whence"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn lseek
changes the offset of the open file description referred to by
.Fa fd .
Descriptors made by duplication or inherited across
.Xr fork 2
share this offset.
The new position is determined by
.Fa offset
and
.Fa whence :
.Bl -tag -width "SEEK_END"
.It Dv SEEK_SET No (0)
Set the offset to
.Fa offset
bytes from the beginning.
.It Dv SEEK_CUR No (1)
Add
.Fa offset
to the current position.
.It Dv SEEK_END No (2)
Add
.Fa offset
to the current file size or raw disk window size.
.El
.Pp
Other origins are not implemented.
A negative displacement is permitted if the resulting position is not
before the beginning.
File and raw disk offsets are checked before addition or subtraction;
an out-of-range result is refused without changing the offset.
For an ordinary file, a seek relative to the current position or end
uses the position or size while holding the shared description's lock.
.Ss Devices and limits
Raw disk windows accept 64-bit seeks, including positions beyond the
end of the window.
The 64-bit call permits file and raw disk positions through
9223372036854775807.
The older 32-bit call cannot return a position above 2147483647 and
refuses it with
.Er EOVERFLOW .
.Pp
The boot log is seekable, through position 4294967295 with either call,
and a read of it holds the description's lock, which its seek takes, as a
read of a
.Pa /proc
file does, below.
Seeks on
.Pa /dev/null ,
.Pa /dev/zero ,
and
.Pa /dev/full
always return position 0, ignoring the displacement and origin.
Pipes, the console, the mouse, and other device nodes, including
.Pa /dev/random
and
.Pa /dev/urandom ,
are not seekable and return
.Er ESPIPE .
Other descriptor types, including sockets, pseudo-terminals and the
framebuffer, return
.Er EBADF .
.Ss Directories and proc files
The offset of a directory is the place in its listing that
.Xr getdents64 2
reads next, which that call reports after each entry as the entry's
.Va d_off .
.Dv SEEK_SET
and
.Dv SEEK_CUR
move it, which is how
.Fn rewinddir
and
.Fn seekdir
work;
.Dv SEEK_END
is
.Er EINVAL ,
as it is on Linux's tmpfs.
A directory of
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
takes the place of any entry through 2147483647.
A directory of a file system takes 0 and each place its listing gives,
which the kernel finds by reading the listing from its start, and no
other place, since what a place means is the file system's own; Linux
takes any place and leaves it to the file system.
A place a listing gave may be refused once entries have been added or
removed since.
.Pp
A
.Pa /proc
file seeks the same way, to any place through 4294967295.
Its offset is a byte of its text, or for
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
an address.
The text of a
.Pa /proc
file is the snapshot its open took, except for
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_score_adj ,
whose text each read makes from the value as it is then;
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
has no text, and each read or write reaches the memory as it is then.
.Pp
A seek of a directory or a
.Pa /proc
file reads and sets the offset under the description's lock.
A read or a write of a
.Pa /proc
file holds that lock for its length, as Linux holds such a file's
position lock, so a seek through a descriptor that shares the description
waits for a transfer in progress and moves the offset on from where the
transfer left it.
.Ss System call interface
Musl's
.Vt off_t
is 64 bits on i386, and its
.Fn lseek
uses system call 140,
.Nm llseek
.Pq also called Li _llseek .
Musl declares no separate
.Nm llseek
function.
EBX is the descriptor, ECX and EDX are the high and low 32-bit words of
the signed displacement, ESI points to the 64-bit result, and EDI is
the origin.
This call returns 0 and writes the new position through the result pointer.
A null result pointer is refused for files, raw disk windows, and the
boot log; the three devices that always seek to zero accept it.
A failed copy to a nonnull result pointer can occur after the offset
has changed.
.Pp
The older system call 19 takes the descriptor in EBX, a signed 32-bit
displacement in ECX, and the origin in EDX.
It returns the new 32-bit position directly.
Both kernel calls return a negative error number on failure.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn lseek
returns the new offset from the beginning on success.
On failure it returns
.Li (off_t)-1
and sets
.Va errno .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/fdstale.c
unlinks an open file and seeks to its end through the retained descriptor.
It checks both system call 19 and the 64-bit call used by musl, requiring
each to report the original file size.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOVERFLOW]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is invalid, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
its type is not supported by the seek handler, or the ordinary file
or directory description no longer identifies a valid file.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The 64-bit result cannot be written to user memory, or a required
result pointer is null.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The origin is unsupported, the resulting position would be negative,
a boot-log position exceeds 4294967295, or the file system cannot
supply the file's attributes.
The origin is
.Dv SEEK_END
on a directory or a
.Pa /proc
file, the position is not a place in a directory's listing, or it is
past 4294967295 on a
.Pa /proc
file.
The devices that always seek to zero do not check the origin.
.It Bq Er EIO
The file's metadata could not be read or its device was removed.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
A raw disk window was withdrawn while its descriptor remained open.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The resulting position of a file, a raw disk window or the boot log
exceeds the call's representable range.
.It Bq Er ESPIPE
The descriptor refers to a pipe, the console, the mouse, or a device
without a seek operation.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr getdents64 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr disk 4
